  contains the same  essential genetic code  also the energy currency is all the same  atp and the cellular membrane with a  proton motive force  however we all know that there's a huge  diversity on earth of life  and in fact most of that diversity is in  prokaryotes and archaea and bacteria uh  of course viruses as well uh allow me to  add that  after the last two  uh three presentations  uh so there are an estimated 10 to the  12 species of  prokaryotic cells  and the  arrows show the three domain view of  life  and so the extant life here on earth are  either eukaryotes or  and bacteria  how these formed is still in the realm  of  the unknown  they probably evolved pretty early after  the formation of the earth maybe about  four billion years ago  and we really don't know what happened  during that first  half  the first two billion or so years  but we know that there must have been a  cellular form that established itself  luca and that cellular form had the  genetic code it had chemiosmotic  coupling and then soon afterwards it  must have developed a genome  and developed metabolism like phototroph  respiration photosynthesis synthesis etc  so how do we address uh these kinds of  questions what happened during the first  two billion years of our planets  of life's  evolution well  one way is to study these diverse  extremophiles and i just listed a few of  these on this slide and  the first is our zero files these grow  in a very dry places like the atacama  desert  they they use very little they need very  little water for survival  psychophiles such as uh  one that i'll be talking about halo  reuben like us for funding is from  antarctica and can grow at sub-zero  temperatures  and it also  ends up being also a halophile which uh  are  tolerant of very high salinity in fact  they can survive  in  salt that's  250 million years old  and then there are other  limits to life that  people are studying such as  uh alkaline conditions alkalophiles  acidophiles  and then  there are some very uh well-known  methanogens thermophiles and  hyperthermophiles that  microbiologists also study  so these organisms are so fascinating  that they were  one of the reasons why genomic  technologies were developed and also  genomic technologies were developed  using some of their uh molecules  and so this is a timeline taken from an  asm news which is a precursor to microbe  that you might be familiar with american  society for microbiology it had actually  a foldout of the cover showing a  timeline of genome sequencing so  historically the first genome of course  was in 1995 muscles influenza  however the ones that are boxed those  are all extremophiles so you can see in  the first five or so years  uh about a third of the  three dozen or so genomes that were  sequenced were extremophilic genomes and  that was because of the  extreme interest in uh early evolution  origin of life  so  we  started our project immediately after  the haemophilus project and it took us  about five years to complete it  uh which may surprise some of the  students in the audience that it took  five years he published it in uh just  before  2001 in october of 2000  and we had about uh 50 authors and from  12 different institutions  so the organism that we sequence taylor  bacterium is a halophilic archaea and it  is a poly extremophile like most of  these so not only is it able to grow in  saturating  concentrations of salt  it's also able to tolerate long periods  of desiccation  low oxygen tension  high solar irradiance  and  sometimes elevated or freezing  temperatures and the genome sequence  genome map behind it just shows our  first  fold-out genome that we published in the  proceedings the national academy of  sciences  all of these  extremes are also present on  mars  there's no oxygen really but otherwise  it's very similar so uh there was a lot  of interest right away in  whether  these hilophilic archaea could  survive on  on mars excuse me  so um  this is one of the figures that we  published an integrated view of the  biology and you can see it's it's  complex  we were able to get essentially a  bird's-eye view of the biology of the  organism and i would like to go through  some of the details  during the next few minutes  so one of the most interesting results  that we found  was that the proteins predicted by the  genome  were  unimodal in distribution with respect to  their pi or electronic uh isoelectric  point  uh the red uh peak shows that  the halobacterium proteins are  essentially all very acidic they have a  average pi of under  five  and uh that's in contrast to all of the  other organisms which have a bimodal  distribution of proteins both acidic and  basic with an essentially neutral  proteome  so why is this well  because of the high salinity inside of  the cell  there's actually a reduced concentration  of water it's about 25  salt by weight and what that does is it  results in  what's uh you may know about is salting  out  so the salts dissolve the water that  causes crowding of the proteins and then  the proteins would precipitate out  uh the way in which the cells or the  proteins prevent themselves from  precipitation is through mutual  repulsion caused by the surface negative  charges or the negative charges on the  protein  so here is a structure of a protein that  we've been studying for  a few years now it's a it's a family 42  beta galactosidase and uh you can see  the red acidic and blue  basic residues and in comparison to a  non-halophilic thermos thermophilus  enzyme  and i think you can see that there are  more red and fewer blue and in fact the  charge on the halo archaeal protein  is about  minus 65 as opposed to -4 for the  thermos protein  so this  allows both mutual repulsion and also  binding more tightly to  the water that's available  so if you look at the activity of  proteins like this they're of interest  from a biotechnological perspective  the proteins can function usually  optimally function  in close to saturated uh sodium chloride  or potassium chloride concentrations  and they as a result of their type  binding to water they can also  function in organic solvents  so this is a very unusual and  interesting and potentially useful  property that  may be used for  development of new catalysts  another  interesting result from our first genome  was that  the three domain view of life was  essentially confirmed so the the tree is  an unrooted tree of life using  uh the small ribosomal rna this was  pioneered by carl rose  uh showed that  the hypothesis was that uh prokaryotes  are divided into two  uh the archaea and the bacteria and they  are distinct from the higher organisms  that you carry on the left  in the bottom  on the left  uh  tree however  we do see the same uh three domains of  life however there's some interesting uh  differences the purple  circle is showing the hilophile the  halophile here seems to be a branch of a  methanogen here however over here the  halophile is coming out much closer to  the root what would be considered the  root of the tree if you had rooted it  so  the halo bacterium actually changes its  position it's it has a dynamic phylogeny  if you will  and uh  the we did some additional work with  this uh looking at different bacteria  and uh in this case we took a  cyanobacterium dinococcus radiodurans  and uh a uh  ilicus  and we found that halobacterium is this  deeply branching hello  branching  archaeon excuse me  however if you use bacillus pseudomonas  and e coli it actually becomes a  bacterium  so why is that  well it turns out that there are a whole  lot of bacterial genes in our  halobacterium genome right here  and uh more than in any of the other  archaea similarly there are some  bacteria over here which have archaeal  genes  dermatoga is one of these  so these types of fine endings raise the  that  not all genes in a particular organism  are  original  originally from that  lineage and some of them have been  inherited not by vertical  transmission but by horizontal  transmission so these are presumably  horizontally transferred genes  so those are pretty interesting uh  results that we got early on uh and i've  been doing bioinformatics for a long  time so uh this that really went back to  the last century and i had to show you  this  uh these uh  figure from nih showing  uh the different generations of uh  genome sequencing so the genome  sequencing cost has been of concern for  a long time and it's been going down  quite fast  and in electronics we know of the  moore's law it's a reduction every every  two years and that happened  with  sanger sequencing for a while  and then of course uh illumina and a  whole bunch of other genome sequencing  methods came in second  gen  and uh although the costs dropped  the quality also wasn't quite  the same as with sanger and then now we  have the third gen and the cost  according to the latest  from is uh down to a thousand dollars  per human genome  so of course for a micro genome that's  even much less  and here is a figure from gold showing  that there are something like 350 000  prokaryotic genomes in the database at  ncbi now  so as soon as there were more genomes  available and we also contributed to  some additional genomes we started doing  something that roman taurusov  initiated at nh  and that was reciprocal blasting so this  was the idea was to take one protein  from a genome and blast it against the  second one  and do the reciprocal blast as well so  this is the computationally intensive  but it results in some very interesting  results which is that you find what are  called reciprocal best hits and those  reciprocal best hits allow you to  form clusters of  proteins  or orthologous groups called cogs  we did this for  the halo archaea and found  that there are about 800  uh  core  proteins so these are proteins that have  presumably the same function their  orthologs in the two different organisms  and you can see that many  however there are some that are poorly  characterized or even that there are no  associated in this case no associated  bacterial  clusters of orthologous groups so these  are essentially signature genes that  means these proteins are present in all  the halo archaea but nothing else  so they're in fact a signature how these  organisms perhaps survive  then there's the concept of the pan  genome so there are many other genes  or proteins which are not found in all  of the genomes in fact the largest  number  are unique  and if you look at that of the  forty four hundred  halo alkyl orthologous groups  uh about thirty four hundred thirty six  hundred are in the pan genome  so this is the total  uh gene  content  and that  is  the most interesting part because that's  in the realm of the unknown  so  uh we wanted to get more into more  detail uh analysis of the genomes of  these organisms  and  as several have mentioned it's a  combination of both experimental and  bioinformatics  i'm not going to be able to go into the  details but just give you a couple of uh  examples  of what we've done  so  the  physiology of hillary is very  interesting from the standpoint of  growth in different concentrations of  oxygen and also with light they  photophosphorylate because they have  these membrane pumps that pump protons  and allow the cells to produce atp  they can also grow  by both aerobic and anaerobic  respiration and they also have substrate  level phosphorylation so they're able to  tolerate different concentrations of  oxygen and these properties again i  won't be able to go into in detail but  they're very unique and uh  useful so for instance  when they're photo  photo  growing phototrophically  they produce these intracellular  nanoparticles called gas vesicles which  allow them to float and we're using  these nanoparticles as a vehicle for  vaccines and for drug delivery  then these purple membrane patches are  also being used for a variety of other  biotechnological applications  but with regard to bioinformatics we  initiated uh transcriptomic analysis  during the dna microarray  era  and we for instance tested the uh  gene expression under anaerobic  respiration versus aerobic respiration  fermentation versus aerobic respiration  or anaerobic respiration versus  fermentation and we saw changes in  different genes  and to make a long story short  if you look on the right  essentially the cells are have a  hierarchy of responses from  aerobic respiration  when the redox goes down it goes to  bacteriodopsin based phototrophy  then  a lower redux  uh if the there's a certain uh  compounds available for anaerobic  respirations like dmso tmao not in some  cases nitrate or even perchlorate  they can uh  survive by anaerobic respiration  and if there's um if there are no uh  terminal electron acceptors at all but  there are some substrates like arginine  they can still survive through  fermentation  so they're quite versatile organisms in  this way  the other very interesting property of  hilo archaea is that  they are incredibly radiation resistant  so you can see here the the killing  curves  uh they're almost as uh tolerant as  dinococcus ray endurance in terms of uv  light and uh they're able to repair uv  light with the photolyze uh and i won't  go into the details in this figure but  basically they have a photo liase that  allows them to re do direct photo repair  they also have a uvr abc  to repair  a do darker pair of uvv damage  and so here again we've mutated the  evr abc and you can see that the  cells are not able to survive and we  could do transcriptional or we did do  transcriptomic results and we could see  which gene uh genes are actually  affected by uv light and you can see  it's a very small number so the cis  system is very highly developed  uh  in terms of being able to  survive uv light  but um it does not seem to resemble the  bacterial sos type response  and then uh  hillary are also resistant to ionizing  radiation and this was a bit of a  surprise although ionizing radiation of  course results in double-stranded breaks  and that is something that they probably  have to deal with when they are  desiccated because desiccation also  results in that so the the  gel on the left shows you  after ionizing radiation the genome  becomes broken into about 20 pieces  and then after one or two generations  they stitch that back together and  they're perfectly fine  so we went through a uh experiment where  we treated with a  killing dose of  ionizing radiation in this case we use  linac  and then we allow them to recover and we  did this three or four times and then we  isolated mutants and tested their  ability to tolerate ionizing radiation  and what we found is very interesting  shown here which is the uh survival  fraction increased quite dramatically  so these are the the mutants that had  survived these multiple rounds of uh  killing doses  and you can see that they're actually  more resistant than dinococcus  radiodurans  so they also have a very interesting  way of  uh being able to tolerate ionizing  radiation and our transcriptomic results  showed that they upregulate only one  operon and that codes for a eukaryotic  type of single-stranded dna binding  protein  so we've done some additional work on  this to show that over expression of  these proteins actually results in  increased radiation resistance so this  may be of interest uh going forward as  we consider sending uh astronauts to  mars  so finally  uh the last thing i want to tell you  about is are some experiments to see  whether halo archaea could actually  survive on mars  so the closest we could get to mars  was uh earth's stratosphere and this is  basically showing  the atmospheric pressure  ozone uh  h2o and temperature  uh higher in this in the atmosphere this  is the troposphere the the stratosphere  is maybe 30 kilometers up  it's very similar to mars up there there  are to the surface of mars  the temperatures are similar has similar  ev flux elevated cosmic  rays electrons protons very low pressure  and it's very dry  so we did an experiment with  collaborators in california  uh where we  sent cultures by airplane to california  from which a point that they launched  them into the stratosphere  and then they recovered it and then they  sent it back to us for analysis  so uh again to make a long story short  we we tested two different hillarq  the  h laccis profundi which as i mentioned  before  is a  cold adapted organism actually it was  isolated from  antarctica  and our lab strain nrc1 and you can see  that after the balloon flight  there is survival in both cases however  if you look at the uh the difference  here  nrc1  sorry uh hilar ruben lux friendly versus  nrc1 you can see that the hilary uber  and lacaze better able to survive uh  stratospheric condition so this one  would presumably do better on mars and  the reasons for that are in the paper  and again i'm going to have to skip over  it but i encourage you to go and take a  look  and very very  fine thing is that there are a lot of  perchlorate salts these very uh  oxidizing salts which are actually used  for  oxidizer for rocket fuel  on the surface of mars  it's very toxic of course to most living  organisms but it turns out that  paleo bacteria are able to survive  tolerate them pretty well and in fact  with halorubrum they're actually able to  grow using perchlorate as the terminal  electron acceptor  so we also have transcriptomic results  from these experiments  so um finally  i just want to leave you with uh  the picture of  nasa and issa and also the  chinese  space administration  they're all  going to uh mars uh nasa and esa are  collaborating on this  mars 2020 as i mentioned uh is currently  has has delivered perseverance rover and  ingenuity  the helicopter to the surface of mars  you may have seen some spectacular  pictures  they're drilling samples so this is a  pretty elaborate um set of plants here's  the earth here's mars uh so this rover  is on mars it's collecting samples it's  going to collect samples from several  different places in a in a an ancient  river delta called  jezero  and it's going to put these samples in  in various places  to be picked up by a second rover which  is going to deliver it to  a spot from which it will be returned  back to earth  and it's going to uh  i believe it's going to be analyzed in a  laboratory in the u.s  and um  so we may very well be able to learn  whether there was ever life on mars  this particular  mission which will take as i said 10  years is going to be  for ancient life  so  the river delta is a place where they  would find sediments that might have  preserved some  signatures of life  however  some of us that  have gone to nasa meetings where there's  also discussions about other missions to  look for extent life  and uh  there are four areas where uh scientists  feel that there may in fact be extant  life on mars  uh that includes uh ice both polar  and mid-latitude ice  salt deposits which of course is of  great interest to us  and also subsurface such as in lava  tubes  caves  and then there are a lot of people who  are very interested in the deep  subsurface there's a lot of water in the  deep south surface about a kilometer  below the surface of mars and so  there may in fact be some  uh  extant organisms that might be found  there of course you have to drill down a  kilometer to get to that location and by  the way  if they were there uh the subsurface  water is also extremely salty so uh they  would probably also be halophiles  so um  then i just want to conclude by saying  that uh astrobiology is i think a very  interesting new science uh with some  very good practical applications and the  need for bioinformatics  and it's informed at the present time  until we get samples from mars by many  extremophilic forms on here on earth  the genome sequencing in the omics  revolution have provided insights into  the limits of life of these  extremophilic  forms  our focus has been the halo archaea  but  these and other extremophilic genomes  have led to concepts like how proteins  can function in  extreme conditions horizontal gene  transfers and evolution of life  and core and pan genomes  and then hillary cure adapted to poly  extremophilic conditions including the  ones that i just discussed about  low oxygen  high radiation  cold temperatures and  perchlorates  and halophiles are considered as a model  microbes  for survival and martian stressors  and just finally  astrobiology is fascinating however  we also need to make a living here on  earth so i just want to mention  that  there are some real revolutions in  biotechnology including of course  the discovery of thermostable  polymerases which was  probably the biggest development however  there are many others such as the ones  i've listed up here  so  i just want to end there and uh thank uh  the organizers and and uh the audience  for your attention and there have been  our lab has been in operation since 1986  so we've had a lot of people who've gone  through the lab i especially want to  thank  priya dasarma who has been a close  collaborator for 30 years and uh  and uh all of the other lab members and  of course the funding has been uh  absolutely essential for our work and uh  right now we have funding from nasa and  nih and  in the past we've had quite a lot of  funding from nsf  and if you're interested in our genomes  i encourage you to go to our website  which is halo.umbc.edu  so i'll stop there and  i don't know if they're time for  questions but i'd be happy to answer  them
 

